\section{The range of rho}{

\eqn{I - \rho W} is invertible and its determinant positive on the
interval \eqn{(1 / \lambda_{min}, 1 / \lambda_{max})}, the default
interval of \code{spatialreg::lagsarlm()}. frmtmb estimates \eqn{\rho} on
that interval through a logistic map, starting at 0. brms 2.23.0
bounds \eqn{\rho} by \eqn{(\lambda_{min}, \lambda_{max})} instead.
For a row-standardized \code{W} (\eqn{\lambda_{max} = 1}) that is a
narrower interval with the same upper end, which cuts off the
negative values below \eqn{\lambda_{min}}. For a \code{W} that is not
row-standardized, brms's interval reaches past
\eqn{1 / \lambda_{max}}, where the determinant changes sign; there
brms's log-determinant is the log of a negative number and Stan
rejects the proposal, so its support is frmtmb's interval there.

The consequence on a row-standardized \code{W}: when the data favor rho
below the smallest eigenvalue, frmtmb and brms fit different models.
On a row-standardized 8 x 8 queen lattice (eigenvalues -0.5006 to 1)
with data drawn at rho = -0.8, frmtmb's estimate is -1.103, with a
log-likelihood 3.74 above the best inside brms's bounds, and brms's
posterior piles at its bound, -0.5006 (median -0.443; a fifth of the
draws within 0.02 of it).

A \code{W} whose extreme eigenvalue is repeated in all but one direction,
as on a complete graph, gives a likelihood with no maximum at that
end when the model has an intercept; frmtmb warns.
}

\section{What else the term does}{

Only \code{gaussian()} and \code{student()} take it, with a constant \code{sigma}
and \code{nu}, as in brms ("SAR models are not implemented when
predicting 'sigma' or 'nu'"). It is written in the formula of \code{mu}.
The likelihood is one joint density over all the rows, so
\code{weights()}, \code{cens()}, \code{trunc()}, \code{se()}, \code{mi()}, \code{rescor = TRUE},
mixtures and \code{quadrature} are refused, and a response carries one
residual correlation term. Random effects work.

\code{summary()} and \code{\link[frmtmb:confint_varcorr]{frmtmb::confint_varcorr()}} report \code{lagsar}
or \code{errorsar} with a delta-method interval. \code{\link[=fitted]{fitted()}} and
\code{residuals()} use the mean above; pearson residuals divide by the
marginal standard deviation, \eqn{\sigma \sqrt{[(A'A)^{-1}]_{ii}}}.
\code{\link[=simulate]{simulate()}} and \code{\link[=predict]{predict()}} draw the whole response through the
model. \code{\link[frmtmb:autocor_matrix]{frmtmb::autocor_matrix()}} returns \eqn{(A'A)^{-1}}.
\code{\link[=fitted]{fitted()}}, \code{\link[=predict]{predict()}} and \code{\link[=simulate]{simulate()}} take \code{newdata} with one row
per row of \code{M}, read as the locations in their order, as brms takes
it: a counterfactual such as \code{x + 1} everywhere gives the lag model's
spillover, \eqn{A^{-1} \eta_{new}}. When the fit dropped rows for
missing values, \code{newdata} covers them too, and A is then over every
row of \code{M}, at the fitted rho; a missing predictor gives \code{NA}, which
the lag form spreads to every row, as in brms. Other \code{newdata} is
refused; brms refuses it too unless a matrix over the new rows comes
in its \code{newdata2}.
\code{\link[frmtmb:frm_linpred]{frmtmb::frm_linpred()}} gives the linear predictor \eqn{\eta} on any
new data.

brms's class priors \code{lagsar} and \code{errorsar} work:
\code{set_prior("normal(0, 0.5)", class = "lagsar")} is a density on rho,
carried to its internal parameter with the log-Jacobian of the map.
frmtmb.sample's default is brms's, flat on rho over its interval.
The pointwise \code{log_lik()} is refused, as for frmtmb's other residual
covariance terms; brms's is a leave-one-out conditional density.
}

\examples{
set.seed(2)
grid <- expand.grid(r = 1:6, c = 1:6)
n <- nrow(grid)
A <- outer(seq_len(n), seq_len(n), function(i, j) {
  as.numeric(abs(grid$r[i] - grid$r[j]) + abs(grid$c[i] - grid$c[j]) == 1)
})
W <- A / rowSums(A)
d <- data.frame(x = rnorm(n))
d$y <- as.vector(solve(diag(n) - 0.5 * W, 1 + 0.5 * d$x + rnorm(n)))
fit <- frm(bf(y ~ x + sar(W, type = "lag")), family = gaussian(),
           data = d, data2 = list(W = W))
summary(fit)
}
